WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.746
This chart is called a porkchop plot.

00:00:02.746 --> 00:00:05.195
It looks like a folded pork chop.

00:00:05.195 --> 00:00:12.246
It is the most efficient way ever invented to argue with the solar system about when to launch.

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:18.480
Every contour line is a constant amount of energy required to go from Earth to Mars.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.636
The horizontal axis is the launch date — when you leave.

00:00:22.636 --> 00:00:27.015
The vertical axis is the arrival date — when you get there.

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:30.058
At the bottom of the C, energy is lowest.

00:00:30.058 --> 00:00:31.839
That's the cheap window.

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:37.331
The further out you push from the bottom of the C, the more fuel you need.

00:00:37.331 --> 00:00:40.819
The more fuel you need, the heavier the rocket.

00:00:40.819 --> 00:00:45.569
The heavier the rocket, the more expensive everything else gets.

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:49.726
Mars's cheap window opens once every twenty-six months .

00:00:49.726 --> 00:01:08.206
Twenty-six months because that's how long it takes for Earth — which orbits the Sun once a year — and Mars — which orbits once every one point eight eight years — to line up in the geometry where a transfer ellipse from one to the other is shortest.

00:01:08.206 --> 00:01:09.542
Look at the chart.

00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:11.471
Trace the bottom of the C.

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:12.956
Notice how it bends.

00:01:12.956 --> 00:01:19.561
The cheapest day to leave isn't a single instant — it's a window two or three weeks wide.

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:23.569
Now look at the next opening, twenty-six months later.

00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:28.764
The C-shape is in the same place, but the energy values are different.

00:01:28.764 --> 00:01:32.029
Because Mars's orbit isn't a perfect circle.

00:01:32.029 --> 00:01:34.256
Some launch windows are cheap.

00:01:34.256 --> 00:01:35.740
Some are very cheap.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:37.076
Mariner 4 in 1964.

00:01:37.076 --> 00:01:38.189
Viking in 1975.

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:39.600
Pathfinder in 1996.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.935
Curiosity in 2011.

00:01:40.935 --> 00:01:42.494
Perseverance in 2020.

00:01:42.494 --> 00:01:47.912
Every Mars launch in history sits on the bottom of one of these C-shapes.

00:01:47.912 --> 00:01:51.548
The solar system doesn't care what you can build.

00:01:51.548 --> 00:01:54.740
It cares what date your launch pad is open.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:02:00.009
The porkchop plot tells you which dates the solar system has agreed to.

